<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fugger family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fugger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fugger family was a merchant and banking dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. Grown wealthy from textiles, mining and finance in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, they lent heavily to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, notably financing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>'s election as emperor in 1519.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its fairs, alongside those of Bergen op Zoom, attracted English cloth merchants, South German financiers such as the <b>Fuggers</b> and, from about 1500, the Portuguese spice trade, and in 1531 the city opened a purpose-built exchange.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Fugger</b> banking house of Augsburg, under Jakob Fugger, became the most powerful financial enterprise in Europe in the early sixteenth century, funding the election of Charles V in 1519 and lending to the papacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Rome, Pope Leo X authorised a campaign of indulgence preaching in the lands of Albert of Brandenburg, archbishop of Mainz and Magdeburg, under an agreement that half the proceeds would repay the loan Albert had taken from the <b>Fugger</b> bank to pay the papacy for holding his sees.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor, won the imperial election of 1519 with money lent by the <b>Fugger family</b> of Augsburg, and Augustus II the Strong, Elector of Saxony, became a Catholic in 1697 in order to stand for the Polish throne.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Textile production and banking made Augsburg one of Europe's richest cities; the <b>Fuggers</b> financed emperors and founded the Fuggerei social housing in 1516.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-augsburg">Free Imperial City of Augsburg</a></li></ul></section>
